(window.DOM_CHAPTERS=window.DOM_CHAPTERS||{})["co-measurement"]="<h1>Measuring Cardiac Output and the Work of the Heart</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-measure-cardiac-output-instead-of-calculating-it-from-heart-rate-alone\"><span class=\"sec-num\">1.</span> Why measure cardiac output instead of calculating it from heart rate alone?</h2>\n<p>CO = HR × <span data-term=\"stroke-volume\">SV</span>, but at the bedside <strong>stroke volume can't be seen or felt</strong>. A pulse tells you the heart rate, not how much blood each beat pushes out. So we need a way to measure blood flow directly. The lecture's method is <strong>Fick's principle</strong>, which works out flow from how much oxygen the body uses. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output?section=1-what-is-cardiac-output-and-why-does-it-matter\">→ Cardiac Output, §1</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"2-fick-s-principle-working-out-flow-from-oxygen-use\"><span class=\"sec-num\">2.</span> Fick's principle: working out flow from oxygen use</h2>\n<p><strong>The idea, with a delivery van.</strong> Vans carry parcels to a town. Each van arrives with 20 parcels and leaves with 15, so it drops off 5. The town takes in 250 parcels a minute. How many vans pass through each minute? 250 ÷ 5 = <strong>50 vans a minute</strong>.</p>\n<p>Blood works the same way:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Oxygen used by the body each minute (V̇O₂)</strong> is the parcels delivered.</li>\n<li><strong>Oxygen dropped off by each mL of blood (arterial − <span data-term=\"mixed-venous\">mixed venous</span> content)</strong> is the parcels per van.</li>\n<li><strong>Blood flow (cardiac output)</strong> is the number of vans.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"fick-process\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Fick oxygen extraction concept\"><span><strong>Arterial O₂ content</strong><small>O₂ carried into tissues</small></span><b>−</b><span><strong>Mixed venous O₂ content</strong><small>O₂ remaining after tissues</small></span><b>→</b><span><strong>O₂ extracted</strong><small>Amount removed from each unit of blood</small></span></div>\n\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Fick's oxygen principle</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"V̇O₂ = CO × (CₐO₂ − CᵥO₂)\"><span class=\"eq-layout\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">V̇O₂</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">=</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">CO</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">×</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">(</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">CₐO₂</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">−</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">CᵥO₂</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">)</span></span></span></div>\n\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Fick cardiac output</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"CO = (V̇O₂) divided by ((CₐO₂ − CᵥO₂))\"><span class=\"eq-layout\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">CO</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">=</span><span class=\"eq-fraction\"><span class=\"eq-numerator\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">V̇O₂</span></span><span class=\"eq-denominator\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">(</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">CₐO₂</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">−</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">CᵥO₂</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">)</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<p><strong>The lecture's example, in three steps:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Oxygen used:</strong> 250 mL O₂/min</li>\n<li><strong>Oxygen dropped off per mL of blood:</strong> 0.20 − 0.15 = <strong>0.05 mL O₂ per mL of blood</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Flow:</strong> 250 ÷ 0.05 = <strong>5,000 mL blood/min = 5.0 L/min</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Check the units:</strong> (mL O₂/min) ÷ (mL O₂ per mL blood) = mL blood/min. The oxygen cancels and leaves blood flow.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"fick-calculator\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>Fick calculator</figcaption><p>Three sliders: oxygen use (150–1,000 mL/min), arterial O₂ content (0.10–0.22 mL/mL) and mixed venous O₂ content (0.05–0.18 mL/mL; it cannot exceed arterial). The three steps update live, with the answer in L/min. A small bar shows the oxygen dropped off per mL. It starts at the lecture's 250, 0.20 and 0.15 → 5.0 L/min. One line notes the pattern: when tissues take more oxygen from each mL, the same oxygen use needs less flow.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Content, not saturation.</strong> Fick needs the <strong>amount of oxygen in each mL of blood</strong> (content). <strong>Saturation</strong> only tells you what percentage of haemoglobin is carrying oxygen. Someone with anaemia can be 98% saturated but carry much less oxygen per mL, because there is less haemoglobin. Using saturation in place of content gives the wrong answer.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-where-do-the-oxygen-measurements-come-from\"><span class=\"sec-num\">3.</span> Where do the oxygen measurements come from?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Arterial content:</strong> from any systemic artery, since arterial blood is the same everywhere.</li>\n<li><strong>Mixed venous content:</strong> from the <strong>pulmonary artery</strong>, through a catheter.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why the pulmonary artery?</strong> Veins from different organs carry very different amounts of oxygen. The kidney takes little oxygen out, while working muscle takes a lot. Their blood only becomes fully mixed after passing through the right heart. A sample from one arm vein shows only what the arm used.</p>\n<p><strong>Why not use a finger pulse oximeter?</strong> It measures arterial saturation only. It can't measure venous content or blood flow.</p>\n<p><strong>Direct versus estimated Fick.</strong> A true (direct) Fick measures oxygen use at the time. Some clinics use a predicted value instead, which adds error. Either way, the method assumes a <strong>steady state</strong>: oxygen use and flow must be stable while the samples are taken.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"4\" data-part=\"Sections 1–3\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 1–3 · 4 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Oxygen use is 250 mL/min. Arterial content is 0.20 and mixed venous content 0.15 mL O₂ per mL blood. What is the cardiac output?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. 1.25 L/min, from 250 × 0.05 ÷ 10</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. 5.0 L/min, from 250 ÷ 0.05</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. 12.5 L/min, from 250 ÷ 0.02</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. 50.0 L/min, from 250 ÷ 0.005</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"co-measurement:5a1308fd\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> 0.20 − 0.15 = 0.05 mL O₂ per mL blood. 250 ÷ 0.05 = 5,000 mL/min = 5.0 L/min.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why is mixed venous blood taken from the pulmonary artery, not an arm vein?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Arm veins contain no measurable oxygen at all</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Pulmonary artery blood has been oxygenated by the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Blood from every organ has mixed by the time it reaches there</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The pulmonary artery is the easiest vessel to reach</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"co-measurement:dec29d4d\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Organs extract different amounts of oxygen. Only after the right heart is the venous blood from the whole body mixed. Pulmonary artery blood has <strong>not</strong> yet passed through the lungs.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A patient with severe anaemia has 98% arterial saturation. Why can't 98% be used in the Fick formula?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Saturation over 95% always makes the formula invalid</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Fick needs oxygen content, which is low when haemoglobin is low</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Anaemia stops oxygen from leaving the blood at the tissues</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Saturation is only measured in venous blood, not arterial</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"co-measurement:ae273481\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Saturation is a percentage of haemoglobin. With little haemoglobin, each mL carries less oxygen even at 98%.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Oxygen use stays at 250 mL/min, but the tissues now take out 0.10 mL O₂ from each mL of blood instead of 0.05. What does this mean for cardiac output?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It has doubled, to 10 L/min</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It is unchanged, at 5 L/min</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It has halved, to 2.5 L/min</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It cannot be worked out without heart rate</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"co-measurement:a15e6fbc\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> 250 ÷ 0.10 = 2,500 mL/min. When each mL gives up more oxygen, less flow is needed for the same oxygen use. This is what happens when cardiac output falls.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"4-other-ways-to-measure-flow-indicator-dilution-supplementary\"><span class=\"sec-num\">4.</span> Other ways to measure flow: indicator dilution (supplementary)</h2>\n<p>The same &quot;what goes in must come out&quot; idea underlies other methods.</p>\n<p><strong>Dye dilution.</strong> Inject a known amount of dye into a vein, then measure its concentration downstream over time. <strong>Fast flow dilutes the dye more and washes it past quickly</strong>, so the concentration curve is low and short. Slow flow gives a higher, longer curve. From the dose and the curve, flow can be worked out.</p>\n<p><strong>Thermodilution.</strong> The same idea, using <strong>cold</strong> saline instead of dye. A pulmonary artery catheter injects it, and a temperature sensor (thermistor) downstream records how much and for how long the blood cools.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Method</th>\n  <th>What is measured</th>\n  <th>Main pitfall</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Fick</strong></td>\n  <td>Oxygen use, and arterial and mixed venous oxygen content</td>\n  <td>Needs a steady state and good samples</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Dye dilution</strong></td>\n  <td>Dye concentration over time</td>\n  <td>Dye coming round a second time (recirculation)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Thermodilution</strong></td>\n  <td>Blood temperature over time</td>\n  <td>Leaky valves and shunts distort the curve</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture covers Fick only. Dye dilution and thermodilution are standard supplementary methods that use the same mass-balance idea.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"5-what-does-it-mean-for-the-heart-to-perform-work\"><span class=\"sec-num\">5.</span> What does it mean for the heart to perform work?</h2>\n<p>The heart doesn't just move blood. It pushes it <strong>against pressure</strong>, and that takes work. The lecture's simple formula:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Approximate external stroke work</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"SW ≈ SV × P\"><span class=\"eq-layout\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">SW</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">≈</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">SV</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">×</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">P</span></span></span><span class=\"equation-example\">P is the pressure the ventricle pumps against, usually mean arterial pressure. This is an estimate: the exact value is the area of the pressure–volume loop.</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> SV = 70 mL, <span data-term=\"map\">mean arterial pressure</span> = 100 mmHg → stroke work ≈ 70 × 100 = <strong>7,000 mmHg·mL</strong> (about <strong>0.93 J</strong>) per beat.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does the left ventricle do more work than the right?</strong> Both pump the <strong>same volume</strong>, but the left pumps against about <strong>100 mmHg</strong> (aorta) and the right against about <strong>15 mmHg</strong> (pulmonary artery). Same volume against higher pressure means more work. That is why the left ventricular wall is so much thicker. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cv-introduction?section=4-why-is-the-left-ventricular-wall-thicker-than-the-right\">→ Introduction, §4</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"6-visualising-stroke-work-on-a-pressure-volume-loop\"><span class=\"sec-num\">6.</span> Visualising stroke work on a pressure–volume loop</h2>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure work-pv-figure dg-figure\" id=\"cvs08-pv\"><figcaption>Figure 1. Ventricular pressure–volume loop — enclosed area represents external stroke work</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 360 334\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Pressure-volume loop of the left ventricle, running anticlockwise: 1 filling at low pressure from ESV to EDV; 2 isovolumetric contraction, pressure rising at EDV; 3 ejection, volume falling to ESV; 4 isovolumetric relaxation, pressure falling at ESV. The area inside the loop is the stroke work.\"><defs><marker id=\"pv-m\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" class=\"dg-head\"/></marker><marker id=\"pv-b\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" class=\"dg-head-blue\"/></marker><marker id=\"pv-r\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" class=\"dg-head-red\"/></marker><marker id=\"pv-g\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" class=\"dg-head-gold\"/></marker></defs><path d=\"M52 20V236H346\" class=\"dg-axis\"/><path d=\"M130 222C180 228 230 224 272 212V118C252 66 168 60 130 100Z\" class=\"dg-blue-soft\"/><path d=\"M140 223C185 228 228 224 262 214\" class=\"dg-loop\" marker-end=\"url(#pv-b)\"/><path d=\"M272 206V128\" class=\"dg-loop\" marker-end=\"url(#pv-b)\"/><path d=\"M266 110C246 68 172 62 138 94\" class=\"dg-loop\" marker-end=\"url(#pv-b)\"/><path d=\"M130 108V212\" class=\"dg-loop\" marker-end=\"url(#pv-b)\"/><text x=\"201\" y=\"152\" class=\"dg-b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Stroke work</text><text x=\"201\" y=\"172\" class=\"dg-t\" text-anchor=\"middle\">= area inside</text><circle cx=\"201\" cy=\"205\" r=\"11\" class=\"dg-numdot\"/><text x=\"201\" y=\"210\" class=\"dg-numw\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text><circle cx=\"290\" cy=\"165\" r=\"11\" class=\"dg-numdot\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"170\" class=\"dg-numw\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2</text><circle cx=\"201\" cy=\"47\" r=\"11\" class=\"dg-numdot\"/><text x=\"201\" y=\"52\" class=\"dg-numw\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3</text><circle cx=\"108\" cy=\"165\" r=\"11\" class=\"dg-numdot\"/><text x=\"108\" y=\"170\" class=\"dg-numw\" text-anchor=\"middle\">4</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"254\" class=\"dg-s\" text-anchor=\"middle\">ESV</text><text x=\"272\" y=\"254\" class=\"dg-s\" text-anchor=\"middle\">EDV</text><text x=\"344\" y=\"274\" class=\"dg-s\" text-anchor=\"end\">Volume →</text><text x=\"24\" y=\"128\" class=\"dg-s\" text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"rotate(-90 24 128)\">Pressure →</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"302\" class=\"dg-t\">1  Filling</text><text x=\"150\" y=\"302\" class=\"dg-t\">2  Isovolumetric contraction</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"324\" class=\"dg-t\">3  Ejection</text><text x=\"150\" y=\"324\" class=\"dg-t\">4  Isovolumetric relaxation</text></svg><p>Original teaching schematic, not measured patient data. The ventricle fills at low pressure, generates pressure with constant volume, ejects blood as volume falls, and then relaxes at approximately constant end-systolic volume.</p></figure>\n\n<p>Follow the loop round:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bottom:</strong> the ventricle fills at low pressure (volume rises).</li>\n<li><strong>Right side:</strong> it contracts with all valves shut, so pressure rises at constant volume.</li>\n<li><strong>Top:</strong> it ejects (volume falls at high pressure).</li>\n<li><strong>Left side:</strong> it relaxes with all valves shut, so pressure falls at constant volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The area inside the loop is the <span data-term=\"stroke-work\">stroke work</span>.</strong> A taller loop (higher pressure) or a wider loop (bigger SV) means more work. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=8-the-whole-cycle-on-one-map\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §8</a>)</em></p>\n<p>The loop shows only the <strong>outside work</strong> of pushing blood. The muscle also uses energy to pump calcium, keep ion gradients and hold tension, so <strong>stroke work is not the same as the heart's total oxygen use</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-why-does-pressure-loading-increase-the-heart-s-oxygen-demand\"><span class=\"sec-num\">7.</span> Why does pressure loading increase the heart's oxygen demand?</h2>\n<p>The lecture lists three main things that set how much oxygen the heart muscle uses:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Wall tension</strong>, which rises with the pressure the ventricle must develop</li>\n<li><strong>Contractility:</strong> a stronger beat costs more energy</li>\n<li><strong>Heart rate:</strong> more beats per minute</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Pressure work costs more oxygen than volume work.</strong> Raising pressure means building and holding high wall tension, which is expensive. Pumping more volume at the same pressure costs less. The lecture's examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aortic stenosis (pressure load):</strong> the ventricle must generate very high pressure to force blood through a narrow valve, so oxygen demand is high.</li>\n<li><strong>Aortic regurgitation (volume load):</strong> the ventricle pumps a bigger volume each beat, but at near-normal pressure, so oxygen demand rises less.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These are general patterns. Individual patients vary with severity and coronary supply.</p>\n<p><strong>Why must coronary flow rise in exercise?</strong> Even at rest, the heart muscle extracts most of the oxygen from its blood (about 70%). There is little left to take, so when demand rises, <strong>the only way to get more oxygen is more coronary flow</strong>. If narrowed coronaries can't deliver it, the muscle runs short of oxygen and chest pain (<span data-term=\"angina\">angina</span>) results. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/coronary\">→ Coronary Circulation</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Careful:</strong> Fick uses the <strong>whole body's</strong> oxygen use to measure cardiac output. The heart's <strong>own</strong> oxygen use is a separate, much smaller quantity.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"5\" data-part=\"Sections 4–7\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 4–7 · 5 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–7</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> In dye dilution, what does a low, short concentration curve downstream tell you?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Flow is fast, so the dye is diluted and washed past quickly</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Flow is slow, so the dye stays in the vessel for longer</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The dye was injected into the wrong vessel entirely</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The heart rate is too slow for the method to work</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"co-measurement:62ee7468\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Fast flow spreads the same dose through more blood and carries it past quickly, giving a lower, shorter curve.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> The two ventricles eject the same volume. Why does the left do more work?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It ejects blood more slowly than the right does</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It pumps against a much higher pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It has to pump blood through the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Its valves are smaller, so it ejects less</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"co-measurement:ca3be51e\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Stroke work ≈ SV × pressure. The same SV against about 100 mmHg instead of about 15 mmHg means far more work.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> On a pressure–volume loop, what does the enclosed area represent?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The heart's total oxygen use</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The stroke volume</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The external stroke work of one beat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The time for one cardiac cycle</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"co-measurement:e6fec011\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The area is the outside work of pushing blood against pressure. Total oxygen use is larger, and SV is the loop's width.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Which places the greatest oxygen demand on the left ventricle?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Pumping a larger volume at normal pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. A slower heart rate with normal pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. A smaller stroke volume at normal pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Pumping a normal volume against a very high pressure</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"co-measurement:c4fe087c\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> High pressure means high wall tension, the most oxygen-expensive kind of work. That is why aortic stenosis raises demand more than regurgitation.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>5.</strong> Why must coronary blood flow rise when the heart works harder?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Heart muscle already takes out most of the oxygen it receives</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Coronary arteries narrow during exercise</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Blood carries less oxygen during exercise</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Heart rate falls, so each beat needs more flow</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"co-measurement:da25b064\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> With extraction already near its maximum, the only way to get more oxygen is to deliver more blood.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"8-chapter-essentials\"><span class=\"sec-num\">8.</span> Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Fick's principle:</strong> oxygen used per minute = blood flow × oxygen dropped off per mL. So <strong>CO = V̇O₂ ÷ (CaO₂ − CvO₂)</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>The lecture's example:</strong> 250 ÷ (0.20 − 0.15) = 5,000 mL/min = 5.0 L/min.</li>\n<li><strong>Use content, not saturation</strong>: content depends on haemoglobin.</li>\n<li><strong>Samples:</strong> arterial from any artery; <strong>mixed venous from the pulmonary artery</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Fick needs a steady state</strong> and accurate samples.</li>\n<li><strong>Indicator dilution (supplementary):</strong> a known dose of dye or cold saline, measured downstream. Faster flow gives a lower, shorter curve.</li>\n<li><strong>Stroke work ≈ SV × pressure.</strong> On a pressure–volume loop it is the area inside.</li>\n<li><strong>The left ventricle does more work</strong> than the right: same volume, higher pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Heart muscle oxygen demand</strong> rises with wall tension, contractility and heart rate. <strong>Pressure work costs more than volume work.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>The heart already extracts most of its oxygen</strong>, so more demand needs <strong>more coronary flow</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n";
